{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many <a href="/recent-projects/">Cullman homes</a> have downspout issues compromising otherwise well-maintained gutter systems. Older homes often have original configurations adequate when installed but insufficient for current rainfall patterns and our updated understanding of foundation protection. Newer homes may have minimum-code installations that overflow during heavy storms.</p>

<p>Common problems we see: too few downspouts for gutter length (50-60+ feet between them when maximum should be 35-40), undersized 2x3 downspouts that can't handle heavy flow, missing or broken extensions depositing water against foundations, and downspouts positioned where they damage landscaping or create neighbor drainage problems.</p>

<p>Cullman's hilly terrain adds complexity. Water from downspouts doesn't just sit there — it flows downhill. Without proper planning, your drainage becomes your downhill neighbor's problem, or worse, it flows back toward your own foundation.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> Before you spend $2,000+ on new gutters because of overflow, let us look at your downspouts. Half the overflow problems we see in Cullman are downspout issues — not enough of them, wrong size, bad positioning. That's a much cheaper fix than replacing the entire system.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Effective downspout systems have three elements: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and appropriate extensions.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity:</strong> One downspout for every 35-40 linear feet of gutter run, maximum. And they should be 3x4 inch, not 2x3. The larger size handles roughly twice the flow rate. During intense storms, that difference determines whether water drains away or backs up and overflows next to your foundation.</p>

<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Downspouts go where water naturally collects — typically corners and low points in gutter runs. On Cullman's hilly terrain, positioning to work with natural slope makes a real difference — route water downhill away from your home, not toward a neighbor or back around.</p>

<p><strong>Extensions:</strong> Water needs to exit far from your foundation. Extensions should reach 6-10 feet minimum. For Cullman County soil, underground drains routing water 20+ feet away provide the best protection.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Those corrugated black flex pipes people bury in their yards? They clog within 2-3 years. Every time. The corrugations trap debris, roots grow into them, and water stops flowing. If you want buried drainage, use smooth PVC. We dig up failed flex pipe installations constantly.</p>
{% endset %}

